 "drivingQuestion": "From where is the prohibition on gentiles' boiled food derived?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "source supplied by R' Yohanan",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "avodah_zarah.37b.15",
   "daf": "37b",
   "sentenceId": 14,
   "he": "וְהַשְּׁלָקוֹת. מְנָהָנֵי מִילֵּי? אָמַר רַבִּי חִיָּיא בַּר אַבָּא אָמַר רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן: אָמַר קְרָא ״אֹכֶל בַּכֶּסֶף תַּשְׁבִּרֵנִי וְאָכַלְתִּי וּמַיִם בַּכֶּסֶף תִּתֶּן לִי וְשָׁתִיתִי״, כַּמָּיִם — מָה מַיִם שֶׁלֹּא נִשְׁתַּנּוּ, אַף אוֹכֶל שֶׁלֹּא נִשְׁתַּנָּה.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And boiled» vegetables prepared by gentiles are prohibited. The Gemara asks: «From where is this matter» derived? «Rabbi Ḥiyya bar Abba says» that «Rabbi Yoḥanan says: The verse states» that when Moses asked Sihon, King of the Amorites, for passage through his land, he said: «“You shall sell me food for money, that I may eat; and give me water for money, that I may drink”» (Deuteronomy 2:28). By juxtaposing food and water, the verse teaches that food is «like water: Just as» Moses wished to purchase «water that was unchanged, so too,» he wished to purchase «food that was unchanged,» i.e., uncooked. Evidently, this is because foods cooked by gentiles are prohibited."
  }
 ],
